WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.010
- You posted this other adorable
picture of your daughter

00:00:02.010 --> 00:00:02.941
and I love it.

00:00:02.941 --> 00:00:04.410
(audience swoons)
- [Mindy] Oh yeah.

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:05.243
- So here's my thing.

00:00:05.243 --> 00:00:06.180
I can kind of see a little girl

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:07.590
that's maybe dressed with ballet shoes.

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:09.240
Is your daughter at
ballet practice right now?

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:10.409
- Yes. So she's...
(audience laughs)

00:00:10.409 --> 00:00:11.400
you can tell from this wallpaper,

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:12.240
she's at our dance...

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:13.320
look at this wallpaper, by the way.

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:14.540
- Oh, I didn't even
see the wallpaper. Wow.

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:17.370
- So she's at this dance school in L.A.

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:19.650
I mean, she's three, so it's not like

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:21.180
very rigorous or anything,

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:23.610
but it's a time when
like... I love girly stuff.

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:25.920
I love dressing her up in girly stuff

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:26.970
and she hates it.

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:28.320
But I thought, okay,
she's going to ballet,

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:29.580
she's pretty physical.

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:31.110
I got her, like, the tutu,

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:33.360
the little leotard, like everything.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:34.590
Never wears it.

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:38.220
Will only wear this yellow Kobe Bryant

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:39.717
shorts and jersey set.

00:00:39.717 --> 00:00:40.550
(audience applauds)
- I love it!

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:43.470
I love that she's so confident.

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:44.550
None of the other kids are wearing that.

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:45.840
She's just like, this is what I'm wearing.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:47.237
And you're jealous. I'm aware.

00:00:47.237 --> 00:00:51.930
- Yeah! She only... in her
mind, it's only appropriate

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:54.510
to be doing ballet in her favorite...

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:55.343
but you know what?

00:00:55.343 --> 00:00:57.560
Kobe Bryant was a great
Laker, and a great...

00:00:57.560 --> 00:00:59.190
we love him in Los Angeles, so I'm happy,

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:03.810
but I do wish she would
wear something more girly.

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:04.740
I gotta say.

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:05.760
- I have to have this argument.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:06.660
I have this argument with my nanny,

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:08.100
and I'm like "Just let
her wear what she wants."

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:10.020
'Cause my mom just let
me wear whatever I want.

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:11.955
And I think it's kind of a
form of creativity, as well.

00:01:11.955 --> 00:01:12.788
- [Mindy] Yeah, that's a good point.

00:01:12.788 --> 00:01:13.980
And so, I'm just like, all right,

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:15.960
just be you, do you, you'll figure it out.

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:17.670
And my nanny is so funny.

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:18.929
She's like embarrassed

00:01:18.929 --> 00:01:20.700
to be out with her.
(audience laughs)

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:22.560
'Cause some of the stuff she puts on...

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:24.300
definitely had Punky Brewster.

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:25.140
It's like, whoa.

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:26.250
- Don't you feel like you say that?

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:27.390
'Cause I'm also like...

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:30.300
let the child live their
own expressive life.

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:31.133
And then they put
something and you're like,

00:01:31.133 --> 00:01:32.790
"That's heinous. You're not wearing that."

00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:34.530
- I've actually never said that.

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:35.610
- You're such a good mom.

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:36.443
- No, here's...

00:01:36.443 --> 00:01:37.649
it's not like I'm such a good mom...

00:01:37.649 --> 00:01:42.060
I think it was a really
important thing for me to...

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:44.220
that was my way of expressing myself.

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:45.300
And I wasn't a writer yet,

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:47.580
and I wasn't a singer yet,
so much, when I was younger.

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:49.680
So I think it was a way of...

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:50.517
I loved it.

00:01:50.517 --> 00:01:52.410
And I looked like a hot mess.

00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:54.750
And sometimes Riv does
too, but it's awesome.

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:55.583
I love it.

00:01:55.583 --> 00:01:57.153
- My daughter's gonna
watch this and be like,

00:01:57.153 --> 00:01:58.140
"See? Kelly Clarkson says...
(audience laughs)

00:01:58.140 --> 00:01:59.550
that I should wear whatever I want."

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:00.383
- And I'm like, "You will wear a dress."

00:02:00.383 --> 00:02:01.650
- I'm abnormal though.

00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:03.487
'Cause like, even my ex-husband was like,

00:02:03.487 --> 00:02:05.190
"Are they gonna...they're not matching.

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:06.870
Their socks don't match
and their shoes..."

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:08.729
I'm like, "They're fine."

00:02:08.729 --> 00:02:09.703
- That's better.

00:02:09.703 --> 00:02:10.740
Even I should do that.

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:12.000
That's probably the answer.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:13.620
- I don't know if it's the
best thing, but I do it.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:14.910
I should probably read a parenting book.

00:02:14.910 --> 00:02:16.091
It's probably a no no.

00:02:16.091 --> 00:02:16.924
(audience applauds)

00:02:16.924 --> 00:02:19.170
So you started writing
at six. That's amazing!

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:21.810
So what's one favorite
thing you wrote as a kid?

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:23.310
- Okay. Well, when I say writing,

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:24.500
I don't mean like it was so amazing-

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:26.130
- No I know, but you loved it.

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:28.530
- I loved writing since I was very little,

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:30.630
and my mom was a doctor,

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:33.000
and after school I was like
the typical latchkey kid

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:34.710
where instead of going home,

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:36.270
I'd just go to her office

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:38.880
and she'd just put me in
one of her little rooms.

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:39.930
She was an OB-GYN.

00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:41.700
So I was in the room where
they, like, took blood.

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:42.780
- That could be frightening for a girl.

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:46.080
- Yeah. So we were in the room
where they would draw blood.

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:47.700
So, patients would come in and draw blood.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:48.810
And my mom was like, just don't, like,

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:50.070
look at anyone and be weird.

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:51.720
So she gave me a little typewriter.

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:53.880
And then, I would sit in the corner,

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:55.620
and she's like, "You can
either read, or you can write."

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:57.390
There was like, I wasn't
allowed to watch TV,

00:02:57.390 --> 00:02:58.470
I didn't have comic books or anything.

00:02:58.470 --> 00:02:59.340
And so I was like-

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:00.840
- What a great investment she gave you.

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:03.120
Like, wow. You're like
a phenomenal writer now.

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:04.650
- Yeah, although, at
that time, I was like,

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:07.350
I wish that we could just
have like a Nintendo,

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:08.183
like a normal kid.
- Yeah.

00:03:08.183 --> 00:03:10.920
- But anyway, so I would just
write these little plays.

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:12.930
And then, in between my mom's patients,

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:15.870
I'd run out, and a
pregnant woman would leave,

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:17.460
and she'd be like, "Okay,
I have five minutes."

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:20.340
And I would show her a little
page of a play that I wrote.

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:21.990
And so, I wrote this one play

00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:23.550
about a haunted house

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:27.330
where like a werewolf and a
witch and a vampire lived in.

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:28.230
- I love that.

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:29.063
- And I would write all these things,

00:03:29.063 --> 00:03:31.080
and I'd be like, "What do
you think? Are they funny?"

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:32.400
And she was such a tough critic.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:33.233
She would be like, "No."

00:03:33.233 --> 00:03:34.110
- [Kelly] So was my mother!

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:34.943
- Such a tough critic!
- Yeah!

00:03:34.943 --> 00:03:36.420
- And I think it helped me.
- Me too!

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:38.850
And then this one, she
was like, "I love it."

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:40.770
And I remember at that moment being like,

00:03:40.770 --> 00:03:42.360
oh, this pride I feel...
- [Kelly] Yeah!

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:43.890
- Like I just wanted
to chase that feeling.

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:44.723
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:44.723 --> 00:03:45.556
And so I just went back

00:03:45.556 --> 00:03:47.340
to that weird little room in her office,

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:48.960
and I just kept writing stuff.

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:49.793
- I love that, though.

00:03:49.793 --> 00:03:50.666
Speaking of parents, though,

00:03:50.666 --> 00:03:52.230
my mom could be tough on me, too,

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:53.430
and say, "A lot of people have a dream

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:55.080
to do what you wanna do. It's hard work."

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:56.400
She was very realistic.
- [Mindy] Yeah.

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:57.300
- But I think a lot of parents

00:03:57.300 --> 00:03:58.980
should be more realistic with kids.

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:00.570
Obviously support them, and their dreams,

00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:01.920
but like... I don't know.
- [Mindy] Right.

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:03.450
- You see those people that you're like,

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:05.580
oh, everyone told you that
your poo smells like roses

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:06.480
your entire life.
(audience laughs)

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:07.770
Everything you did is magical.

00:04:07.770 --> 00:04:09.420
And they're so entitled.
- Yeah.

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:11.700
- And I think it's good that
we have parents like that.

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:13.260
But you did deserve a Nintendo.

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:14.093
- It's interesting about it, though,

00:04:14.093 --> 00:04:15.690
is it's not modern, right?

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:17.790
'Cause now, I'm sure your
kids, when they go to school...

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:20.340
Exactly. It's like you get
a trophy for participation.

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:22.920
And if I got one of those,
my mom would throw it away.

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:25.590
- Oh my god! I'm a Texan,
we don't believe in that.

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:26.610
- Yeah! Yes.

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:27.443
- We're like...

00:04:27.443 --> 00:04:28.830
'Cause you have to teach
your kids how to lose.

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:29.940
- Yes.
- Yeah. That's it.

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:31.680
I am not being offensive

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:33.120
to kids or parents that think otherwise.

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:34.020
But I just mean,

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:36.210
that is an important lesson for my kid

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:37.043
to learn how to lose.

00:04:37.043 --> 00:04:37.876
Like when we're playing a game,

00:04:37.876 --> 00:04:39.390
I don't let her win all the time.

00:04:39.390 --> 00:04:40.410
You gotta learn how to lose!

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:41.550
'Cause you gotta learn how that feels

00:04:41.550 --> 00:04:44.010
and work through that emotional
state that sucks, you know?

00:04:44.010 --> 00:04:45.270
- Yeah. No, a hundred percent.

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:48.090
- Yeah. Well, this is
a great conversation.

00:04:48.090 --> 00:04:50.640
So you started out as
the only female writer

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:51.473
for The Office.

00:04:51.473 --> 00:04:54.570
Was that like... horrifying or hard?

00:04:54.570 --> 00:04:55.650
It seems awkward.

00:04:55.650 --> 00:04:56.707
If you were walking in there being like,

00:04:56.707 --> 00:04:57.660
"Yeah, my jokes are awesome."

00:04:57.660 --> 00:04:59.545
- Yeah, so this was back in 2004.

00:04:59.545 --> 00:05:00.378
I was 24.

00:05:00.378 --> 00:05:02.100
- That's...a long time ago.
- Long time ago.

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:03.690
And I think there's, like,

00:05:03.690 --> 00:05:04.950
there's been so many changes now,

00:05:04.950 --> 00:05:05.850
but the thing is,

00:05:05.850 --> 00:05:07.860
most of the people who were on The Office

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:08.693
in the writers' room,

00:05:08.693 --> 00:05:09.526
are still my friend now,

00:05:09.526 --> 00:05:11.070
I was really lucky.

00:05:11.070 --> 00:05:12.390
But yeah, I was the only woman,

00:05:12.390 --> 00:05:14.640
only person of color on the writing staff.

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:17.820
But I think it was really useful to me.

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:19.920
Because what I learned was like,

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:23.460
Okay. I just have to impress these guys.

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:25.260
I know I don't necessarily look like them,

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:27.960
but I would show up an hour early,

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:29.820
I'd leave an hour after everyone else did.

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:31.110
Even if I didn't have any work to do.

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:33.240
Just 'cause I wanted to give
the appearance of, like...

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:35.070
I am the hardest working person here.

00:05:35.070 --> 00:05:36.210
- Yeah.
- Because, you know,

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:38.288
I didn't have any
experience writing in TV.

00:05:38.288 --> 00:05:39.570
- And you'd feel like
you have to work harder.

00:05:39.570 --> 00:05:40.860
- And I felt like I had to work harder!

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:43.200
And, I have to say, it was
a great lesson to learn,

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:44.100
'cause I did work harder.

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:45.090
But then, it's like,

00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:46.590
I wrote the most episodes in The Office

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:47.471
of any writer there.

00:05:47.471 --> 00:05:48.698
I wrote 24 episodes.
- That's amazing!

00:05:48.698 --> 00:05:49.710
(audience applauds)
- Thank you.

00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:51.317
- God, what a funny show, too, wow.

00:05:51.317 --> 00:05:52.760
- I think it's like...
it's a double edged sword.

00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:54.630
I feel like, what I tell my daughter,

00:05:54.630 --> 00:05:57.090
and a lot of my friends who are women,

00:05:57.090 --> 00:05:58.650
and women of color, is like...

00:05:58.650 --> 00:05:59.483
if we think of it as like,

00:05:59.483 --> 00:06:01.830
"Oh great, I have to work so
much harder to prove myself,"

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:02.663
but it's like,

00:06:02.663 --> 00:06:05.460
"Oh, great. I have the
opportunity to work harder,

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:07.650
and learn more, and prove myself."

00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:10.230
And so, I think putting
in all that extra time,

00:06:10.230 --> 00:06:12.360
back in the day, really
prepared me for like...

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:13.740
having my own show when I had my own show,

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:15.000
which I'm sure, you know...
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:16.467
- You felt like, "Whoa, I'm
so happy I worked so hard."

00:06:16.467 --> 00:06:18.720
- Oh, I had to be a hard
worker my whole life.

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:19.737
But I think that panned out well.

00:06:19.737 --> 00:06:21.212
- And I also, if I can say this, too,

00:06:21.212 --> 00:06:25.980
I was never this traditionally
beautiful-on-camera person,

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:28.066
and I felt like I was
like, it doesn't matter.

00:06:28.066 --> 00:06:30.390
I have something else,
which is that I can write.

00:06:30.390 --> 00:06:31.830
I can do this other thing.

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:34.320
And it made me... special.

00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:36.690
And so, beauty fades,

00:06:36.690 --> 00:06:39.000
and I'll still be able to be
writing in the corner at home.

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:39.990
- But here's what's funny,

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:40.957
'cause you walked out and I was like,

00:06:40.957 --> 00:06:42.007
"Good gosh, you're beautiful."

00:06:42.007 --> 00:06:43.800
So that's so funny.
- (laughs) I'll take it.

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:45.030
- No, it's how see ourselves.

00:06:45.030 --> 00:06:46.320
'Cause I don't see myself as that, either.

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:48.090
And I always have to make
jokes when people make...

00:06:48.090 --> 00:06:49.200
I feel like I'm Funny Girl,

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:51.030
like Fanny Brice in Funny Girl,

00:06:51.030 --> 00:06:52.050
when they ask her to
sing a beautiful song,

00:06:52.050 --> 00:06:53.760
and she's like... not without humor.

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:55.230
And I'm like, yeah, can't do it.

00:06:55.230 --> 00:06:56.220
And I think that's me too.

00:06:56.220 --> 00:06:58.440
But I think all of that
is character-building,

00:06:58.440 --> 00:07:00.270
and I do think that it
makes you focus on...

00:07:00.270 --> 00:07:01.950
when you aren't, that's not
your focus, you focus on

00:07:01.950 --> 00:07:03.600
your actual gifts and your
talents, and what you really

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:06.407
wanna accomplish. So, I think that's good.

